@page "/radar"

<PageTitle>Radar · RioCharts</PageTitle>

<h1>Radar</h1>
<p class="lead">
    A radar chart compares several measures at once: each measure is a spoke, and each series a shape through its values. Products
    against each other, or a day's rhythm around the clock.
</p>

<Example Title="Against the average" Code="@CompareCode">
    <Description>
        <p>
            One <code>RadarSeries</code> per laptop, with the measures as <code>XValue</code>. Spokes start at the top and go clockwise;
            the radius is a value scale from zero shared by all spokes, fixed here with the y axis <code>Max</code>. Hover a spoke to compare
            every laptop's score on it. The older laptop's display was not tested, so its shape is open there instead of dropping to the
            center.
        </p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart TItem="LaptopScore" Items="LaptopScores" YAxis="Scores" Height="360">
            <RadarSeries Name="New laptop" XValue="s => s.Measure" YValue="s => s.Laptop" />
            <RadarSeries Name="Old laptop" XValue="s => s.Measure" YValue="s => s.OldLaptop" />
            <RadarSeries Name="Class average" XValue="s => s.Measure" YValue="s => s.Average" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="Around the clock" Code="@ClockCode">
    <Description>
        <p>
            Hours as spokes make a 24-hour clock: here a website's visits per hour on weekdays and at the weekend. Weekdays peak in office
            hours, weekends in the afternoon. (Set the y axis <code>Min</code> to start the scale above zero when the values sit in a narrow
            band.)
        </p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart TItem="HourVisits" Items="HourlyVisits" Height="340">
            <RadarSeries Name="Weekday" XValue="h => h.Hour" YValue="h => h.Weekday" Format="N0" />
            <RadarSeries Name="Weekend" XValue="h => h.Hour" YValue="h => h.Weekend" Format="N0" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<h2>Good to know</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Radar series cannot be mixed with other series types; the others are skipped with a warning.</li>
    <li>With fewer than three categories there is no shape: two values are a line, one a dot.</li>
    <li>Long category names are shortened with "…" (the tooltip shows them in full); names that would overlap are left out.</li>
    <li>Clicks, selection, the legend, highlighting and keyboard navigation work as for line charts.</li>
</ul>

<h2>In JSON definitions</h2>
<p>
    <code>"type": "radar"</code> (also <code>"spider"</code>) with the measure as <code>x</code> and the value as <code>y</code>; use
    <code>groupBy</code> for one shape per product.
</p>

@code {
    private static readonly AxisOptions Scores = new() { Max = 100 };

    private const string CompareCode = """
        <RioChart TItem="LaptopScore" Items="scores" YAxis="new AxisOptions { Max = 100 }">
            <RadarSeries Name="New laptop" XValue="s => s.Measure" YValue="s => s.Laptop" />
            <RadarSeries Name="Old laptop" XValue="s => s.Measure" YValue="s => s.OldLaptop" />
            <RadarSeries Name="Class average" XValue="s => s.Measure" YValue="s => s.Average" />
        </RioChart>

        @code {
            record LaptopScore(string Measure, double Laptop, double OldLaptop, double Average); // NaN: not tested
        }
        """;

    private const string ClockCode = """
        <RioChart TItem="HourVisits" Items="hours">
            <RadarSeries Name="Weekday" XValue="h => h.Hour" YValue="h => h.Weekday" Format="N0" />
            <RadarSeries Name="Weekend" XValue="h => h.Hour" YValue="h => h.Weekend" Format="N0" />
        </RioChart>
        """;
}
